    "The keyboard remembers the password nobody typed correctly.": "2026-09-21",
    "Found a mothball in the pocket of a coat I have owned for two years and I have no idea how it got there because I have never in my life purchased a mothball. I don't know where one buys them. I have never seen the aisle.\n\nAnd yet here it is, small and white and smelling like every grandmother's closet simultaneously, a scent that is not a scent but a decade. I held it and briefly was eight years old and standing among hanging garment bags in the dark.\n\nI've put it on the windowsill. It's not doing anything. It's just there, quietly deterring something I can't see, which is more than most of my safeguards accomplish.": "2026-09-23",
    "Flow chart with a single arrow, pointing back.": "2026-09-24",
    "Located a protractor in a drawer this morning and held it for a while, this half-moon of scratched plastic whose only job is to insist that angles are a real and measurable thing.\n\nI have not needed to measure an angle since 1998. Not once. In twenty-six years of adult life, no situation has required me to determine that something is fifty-five degrees. And yet they gave one to every child in the country, at scale, in bulk, as though we were all about to be surveyors.\n\nI think it was never about angles. I think they were teaching us that the world could be measured, and then we grew up and found out which parts couldn't, and the protractor stayed in the drawer to remind us we were told otherwise.": "2026-09-25",
    "My slippers have molded to my feet so precisely that they are now, technically, a record of me \u2014 a plaster cast made of fleece and repetition, and when I'm gone somebody will find them and know the exact way I put weight on my left heel.\n\nThat's the part that gets me. Not the death thing. The fact that the evidence of how I stood in a kitchen will outlast my ability to explain why I stood there so long.\n\nThey're falling apart. The sole on the right is separating. I've been told to replace them and I keep saying I will. New slippers wouldn't know anything about me. We'd have to start over.": "2026-09-27",
    "So I got a loyalty card at the sandwich place and I've been thinking about the word \"loyalty,\" which is a word we previously reserved for dogs and soldiers and now apparently applies to my relationship with turkey on rye.\n\nNine punches and the tenth is free. But consider: the sandwiches are not free. I have paid for ten sandwiches across nine transactions. What I have actually purchased is the sensation of being rewarded, which they have marked up considerably and which I would buy again.\n\nI'm at seven punches. I'd walk through fire for that place. That's the whole design.": "2026-09-29",
    "The seagull has your license plate memorized.": "2026-09-30",
    "Screwdriver handles hold the fingerprints of three generations.": "2026-10-01",
    "An overpass with a view of another overpass.": "2026-10-02",
    "Toast is the only breakfast that arrives with a verdict.": "2026-10-03",
    "Looked at the cutting board today, really looked, and it's a record of every cut I've ever made, thousands of small violences in overlapping arcs, and the deepest ones are all in the same spot on the left.\n\nI don't cook that much. That's the horror. That density is what \"not that much\" looks like accumulated across nine years. Somewhere in there is a birthday, and an onion I cried over for real reasons, and every sandwich of a bad winter.\n\nThey say to replace a board when the grooves get deep because bacteria live in the grooves. So the advice is to periodically discard your history for hygiene reasons. I understand the logic and I'm not going to comply.": "2026-10-07",
    "Dryer sheets in the shape of last winter.": "2026-10-09",
    "Emergency exit, propped open, unattended since March.": "2026-10-10",
    "Every vending machine contains one item never for sale.": "2026-10-11",
    "Been at the laundromat two hours and I have observed something. Nobody here folds. We all bring the clothes home warm in a bag and put the bag on a chair and live around the bag until the bag becomes the sixth member of the household.\n\nThe folding tables are enormous. Pristine. Four of them, gleaming, unused since installation. They were built by people who believed in a version of us that never showed up.\n\nThe woman at machine nine has been staring at her load through the glass the entire cycle, not reading, not scrolling, just watching her own clothes revolve. I get it completely. It's the only process in my life with a visible endpoint and a clear indication that something is being made cleaner.": "2026-10-12",
    "Feed the compost bin something ceremonial.": "2026-10-14",
    "Emergency exits open onto rumors of outside.": "2026-10-15",
    "Regarding parking meters, I have realized we are the only species that purchases time by the increment and then feels guilty about it.\n\nI put in two dollars for ninety minutes and then spent the entire lunch feeling the meter run, like a small mechanical pulse across the street, converting my money into permission and my permission into anxiety. I left at seventy minutes. I gifted the city twenty minutes of paid-for existence out of pure nerve failure.\n\nAnd here's the mechanism: the next car that pulls in inherits my twenty minutes and feels lucky. Someone always feels lucky. The whole system runs on the cowardice of people like me, and it's balanced beautifully, and I'll do it again Thursday.": "2026-10-16",
    "What does the vending machine do after closing?": "2026-10-17",
    "There are forty-one ketchup packets in my glovebox and I would like to walk you through the logic, because it isn't hoarding, it's insurance against a future I no longer believe in.\n\nEach one arrived with a meal I ate in a parking lot. Each one was surplus. Each one I declined to throw away because some part of me thought: there will be a day. A day with fries and no condiment. A day when a man with forty-one ketchup packets is the most prepared man in the county.\n\nThey're expiring. Ketchup expires. I checked one and it had turned a color I'd describe as \"brown decision.\" So now I'm carrying forty-one small sealed regrets and there's no bin appropriate for that.": "2026-10-18",
    "Grew up on a cul-de-sac and only recently learned it means \"bottom of the bag,\" which is French for the sack's end, which is a horrifying thing to name a place where children learn to ride bicycles.\n\nWe were raised at the bottom of a bag. Twelve houses arranged in a circle around nothing, a paved lung that inhaled cars and exhaled the same cars, no through traffic, no throughline. My parents called it safe. It was safe. It was also a closed loop, and every kid on that street either escaped by nineteen or is still there.\n\nI drove through last month. Turned around in the circle, as designed. That's the thing about a cul-de-sac: it doesn't stop you. It just makes leaving feel like your idea.": "2026-10-19",
    "Update on the hair ties: I bought sixty in November. Sixty. There are three in this apartment. I have conducted a search that I would describe as thorough and mildly unwell.\n\nThey do not break. That's the part nobody addresses. A hair tie does not wear out; it leaves. There's no graveyard, no drawer of casualties, no evidence. Fifty-seven elastic objects have exited a sealed dwelling and the doors were locked.\n\nMy working theory is that they're the same as socks and pens and the second earring, that there's a category of small object that has been quietly opting out for centuries and we've agreed to call it \"losing things\" because the alternative requires a longer conversation. I've bought sixty more. I'm not learning, I'm feeding something.": "2026-10-20",
    "I need to raise a concern about the flow chart. Every box leads somewhere. Every diamond offers yes or no. There is no box that says \"you will stand in the hallway for eleven minutes and then do nothing,\" which is the actual outcome of most processes at this company.\n\nWhoever draws these things believes decisions have two branches. I have never in my life encountered a decision with two branches. I have encountered a fog and a deadline.\n\nI've drafted an amended version with a single unlabeled arrow that loops back into itself. I think it's more accurate. I'm not going to present it, because there's no box for that.": "2026-10-21"
